Why Does Dicing Blade Selection Matter More as Wafers Evolve?
Wafer singulation places abrasive tools next to finished circuits, making edge damage and kerf width commercially consequential. A blade must separate dice with acceptable chipping, stable cut geometry, manageable wear and a repeatable coolant recipe. A small change in abrasive size or bond can affect the yield of valuable chips. Procurement therefore weighs cost per acceptable device and line uptime rather than blade purchase price alone.

Mechanical blades remain useful because they offer controlled cutting and established factory workflows. Yet brittle materials, low-k dielectric stacks and dense packages can create reasons to introduce laser grooves or alternative separation processes. Hybrid workflows may retain a mechanical blade for the final cut while reducing damage to vulnerable surface layers. It would be inaccurate to count standalone laser equipment as blade sales.

